            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002207"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002362"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An excitatory granule cell with a soma located in the granular layer of cerebellar cortex. A mature cerebellar granule cell has short dendrites with a characteristic claw-like appearance and a long axon that ascends to the molecular layer where it bifurcates (except in non-teleost fish, where it does not bifurcate) and extends mediolaterally to form parallel fibers.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>BTO:0004278</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ZFA:0001691</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>granule cell of the cerebellum</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#cellxgene_subset"/>
        <rdfs:comment>It has been noted in at least some mammalian species that granule cells are the most numerous cell type in the cerebellum and in the brain, with at least 99% of all cerebellar neurons being granule cells.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>cerebellar granule cell</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/cellguide/CL_0001031</rdfs:seeAlso>
        <skos:prefLabel>cerebellar granule cell</skos:prefLabel>
    </owl:Class>
